    <bpmn:process id="W_W1492" name="W1492. Typhoon Season Pre-Positioning of Emergency Construction Materials (Tarpaulins, Plywood, CGI Sheets) &amp; Demand Allocation Across Store Network" isExecutable="false">
      <bpmn:documentation>Trigger: PAGASA raises typhoon Signal No. 2 or higher over any region where BuildRight operates; also triggered by the annual pre-typhoon season review (May)
Frequency: Annual pre-positioning review (May); event-driven pre-positioning ~8–15 times per typhoon season (June–November); post-typhoon reallocation ~5–10 times/season
Volume: Emergency material stock: ~5,000–10,000 units tarpaulins, ~3,000–5,000 sheets plywood, ~2,000–4,000 bundles CGI sheets pre-positioned across 4 DCs; post-typhoon demand surge: 300–500% normal volume for affected stores
Owner: VP Supply Chain
Participants: Merchandise Planner, DC Manager, Logistics Manager, Store Manager, VP Merchandising
Source: workflow W1492 — Typhoon Season Pre-Positioning of Emergency Construction Materials (Tarpaulins, Plywood, CGI Sheets) &amp; Demand Allocation Across Store Network</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="W1492_LaneSet3">
        <bpmn:lane id="W1492_Lane1" name="VP Supply Chain">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1492_Task5</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1492_Task6</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1492_Start4</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="W1492_Lane2" name="Merchandise Planner">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1492_Task7</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1492_Task8</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>W1492_End9</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:startEvent id="W1492_Start4" name="PAGASA raises typhoon Signal No. 2 or higher over any region where BuildRight operates…">
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1492_Flow12</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1492_Task5" name="Annual pre-typhoon season emergency stock planning">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 1: Annual pre-typhoon season emergency stock planning (May): (a) VP Supply Chain convenes typhoon preparedness meeting with Merchandise Planning and Logistics; (b) review historical typhoon impact data: which regions affected, which stores experienced demand surge, stock-out duration; (c) determine emergency stock targets by material: (i) tarpaulins: based on average roof damage incidents × estimated units per incident; (ii) plywood: based on historical board-up and temporary repair demand; (iii) CGI sheets: based on historical roof replacement demand; (iv) nails, roofing nails, tie wire: complement materials; (d) allocate emergency buffer across 4 DCs proportional to historical typhoon exposure; (e) negotiate advance purchase commitments with key suppliers (plywood mills, CGI sheet manufacturers, tarpaulin importers) for seasonal supply guarantee; (f) emergency buffer budget approved by VP Merchandising
Responsible (R): VP Supply Chain
Accountable (A): VP Merchandising
Duration: 1–2 days</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1492_Flow12</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1492_Flow13</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1492_Task6" name="Event-driven pre-positioning">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 2: Event-driven pre-positioning: When PAGASA raises Signal No. 2+: (a) VP Supply Chain activates typhoon response protocol; (b) system identifies stores in projected typhoon path based on PAGASA track forecast; (c) for stores in high-impact zone: (i) emergency materials expedited from DC to store — maximum 24-hour delivery if store is accessible; (ii) standard replenishment orders for emergency SKUs converted to priority fulfillment; (iii) stores instructed to maximize shelf and yard display of emergency materials; (d) for nearby stores outside direct path: (i) pre-position additional stock as secondary supply points; (ii) these stores serve as replenishment sources for affected stores post-typhoon; (e) Logistics Manager coordinates truck availability for emergency deliveries, prioritizing over routine shipments
Responsible (R): VP Supply Chain / Logistics Manager
Accountable (A): COO
Duration: 4–8 hours per event</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1492_Flow13</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1492_Flow14</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1492_Task7" name="Post-typhoon demand surge allocation">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 3: Post-typhoon demand surge allocation: Within 48 hours after typhoon passes: (a) affected stores report damage assessment and remaining emergency material stock; (b) Merchandise Planner estimates post-typhoon demand by store based on: (i) typhoon severity in store&#x27;s area; (ii) historical post-typhoon sales patterns; (iii) news reports of damage extent; (c) remaining DC emergency stock allocated to stores proportionally; (d) if DC stock insufficient: (i) emergency procurement orders placed with suppliers at pre-negotiated surge pricing; (ii) stock transferred from unaffected stores with surplus (Visayas/Luzon stores may be unaffected while Mindanao stores are hit, or vice versa); (e) allocation priority: (i) stores with complete roof material stock-out (highest revenue impact); (ii) stores in areas with reported widespread housing damage; (iii) remaining stores based on demand forecast
Responsible (R): Merchandise Planner / VP Supply Chain
Accountable (A): VP Merchandising
Duration: 1–2 days post-event</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1492_Flow14</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1492_Flow15</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:userTask id="W1492_Task8" name="Post-season emergency stock disposition">
        <bpmn:documentation>Step 4: Post-season emergency stock disposition (December): (a) remaining emergency buffer stock at DCs and stores counted; (b) if stock is sellable at normal prices: returned to regular inventory allocation; (c) if seasonal-specific items (e.g., heavy-duty typhoon tarps that don&#x27;t sell in dry season): marked down 20–30% or held for next season with proper storage; (d) emergency stock performance review: (i) stock-out incidents during typhoon events; (ii) overstock cost (carrying cost of unsold emergency buffer); (iii) total revenue from emergency material sales during typhoon season; (iv) customer satisfaction data from post-typhoon period; (e) lessons learned feed into next year&#x27;s pre-positioning plan
Responsible (R): Merchandise Planner / VP Supply Chain
Accountable (A): VP Merchandising
Duration: 1 day</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>W1492_Flow15</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>W1492_Flow16</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="W1492_End9" name="Complete">
        <bpmn:incoming>W1492_Flow16</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="W1492_Annotation10">
        <bpmn:text>Controls: - CTL-245 (ensure controlled execution — Supply Chain Orchestration &amp; Risk (PA-02.3).) | - operational: VP Supply Chain review and approval gate
Owner: VP Supply Chain</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
